A locker class gives its description method an extra parameter, so the description never reaches the printer. What shape does the line take?
class Locker {
    int id = 4;
    public String toString(int prefix) { return "L" + prefix + id; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Locker());
    }
}

A label class describes itself by handing back no text at all. What happens?
class Ghost {
    public String toString() { return null; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Ghost());
    }
}

A subclass description wraps the description it inherits from its parent. What prints?
class Base {
    public String toString() { return "Base"; }
}

class Kid extends Base {
    public String toString() { return "Kid(" + super.toString() + ")"; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Kid());
    }
}

A bay object describes itself with no characters at all. What happens?
class Slot {
    int bay = 6;
    public String toString() { return ""; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Slot());
    }
}

A bay class hands back its field from the description method. What happens?
class Bay {
    int n = 3;
    public String toString() { return n; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Bay());
    }
}

A segment class keeps two endpoint objects and describes itself by joining them. What prints?
class Pt {
    int x;
    Pt(int x) { this.x = x; }
    public String toString() { return "(" + x + ")"; }
}

class Seg {
    Pt a;
    Pt b;
    Seg(Pt a, Pt b) { this.a = a; this.b = b; }
    public String toString() { return a + "-" + b; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Seg(new Pt(1), new Pt(4)));
    }
}

A holder class describes itself from a field that no constructor ever assigns. What prints?
class Cell { int n; }

class Holder {
    Cell c;
    int n = 1;
    public String toString() { return c + "-" + n; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Holder());
    }
}

Two tag objects of the same class are built and joined in one line. What prints?
class Tag {
    int id;
    Tag(int id) { this.id = id; }
    public String toString() { return "" + id; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Tag(1) + " " + new Tag(2));
    }
}

A label object is built with one code and then given a different one before it is printed. What prints?
class Label {
    String id;
    Label(String id) { this.id = id; }
    public String toString() { return "Tag:" + id; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Label l = new Label("a7");
        l.id = "z9";
        System.out.print(l);
    }
}

A cell and a liner of different classes are built and joined in one line. What prints?
class Cell {
    int n;
    Cell(int n) { this.n = n; }
    public String toString() { return "" + n; }
}

class Liner {
    String code;
    Liner(String code) { this.code = code; }
    public String toString() { return code; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Cell(4) + ":" + new Liner("L"));
    }
}

A roster class declares three fields and no constructor at all. What prints?
class Roster {
    String label;
    int count;
    boolean active;
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Roster r = new Roster();
        System.out.print(r.label + "|" + r.count + "|" + r.active);
    }
}

A rope class gives a field a starting value and then changes it in the constructor. What integer prints? Respond with the integer only.
class Rope {
    int len = 3;
    Rope() { len = len + 4; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Rope().len);
    }
}

A bed constructor writes to the seed object it is given. What prints?
class Seed { int v = 1; }

class Bed {
    int stored;
    Bed(Seed s) { s.v = 5; stored = s.v; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Seed s = new Seed();
        new Bed(s);
        System.out.print(s.v);
    }
}

A cell class takes an argument in its constructor but never stores it anywhere. What does the program print?
class Cell {
    int n;
    Cell(int n) { }
    public String toString() { return "Cell:" + n; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Cell(4));
    }
}

A bed object is built from a cell object built in the same expression. What prints?
class Cell {
    int n;
    Cell(int n) { this.n = n; }
}

class Bed {
    Cell c;
    Bed(Cell c) { this.c = c; }
    public String toString() { return "Bed(" + c.n + ")"; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Bed(new Cell(2)));
    }
}

Two trays of the same class are built and only the first one is changed. What integer prints? Respond with the integer only.
class Cell { int n; }

class Tray { Cell c = new Cell(); }

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Tray a = new Tray();
        Tray b = new Tray();
        a.c.n = 4;
        System.out.print(b.c.n);
    }
}

A span constructor stores a length that it computes from the value it is given. What prints?
class Span {
    int start;
    int length;
    Span(int s, int len) { start = s; length = len * 2; }
    public String toString() { return start + "+" + length; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(new Span(3, 4));
    }
}

A helper writes to an object that a pair object holds, and the pair was passed in. What prints?
class Cell { int n; }

class Pair {
    Cell first;
    Cell second;
}

class Main {
    static void bump(Pair p) { p.second.n = 9; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Pair p = new Pair();
        p.first = new Cell();
        p.second = new Cell();
        bump(p);
        System.out.print(p.second.n);
    }
}

A helper raises a number it is given, and the caller checks its own variable afterwards. What prints?
class Main {
    static void grow(int n) { n = n + 1; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int size = 2;
        grow(size);
        System.out.print(size);
    }
}

A helper points its own String parameter at different text, and the caller checks its variable afterwards. What prints?
class Main {
    static void fix(String s) { s = "fixed"; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "old";
        fix(a);
        System.out.print(a);
    }
}

A helper appends a mark to a buffer it is given, and the caller prints that buffer. What prints?
class Main {
    static void add(StringBuilder b) { b.append("!"); }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("hi");
        add(sb);
        System.out.print(sb);
    }
}

A helper adds up fields of the object it is given, and the call site passes nothing. What happens?
class Bag {
    String label = "x";
    int n = 3;
}

class Main {
    static int total(Bag b) { return b.n + b.label.length(); }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.print(total(null));
    }
}

A counter object starts at 4 and has to read 9 after the helper runs. Which statement fills the blank?
class Counter { int n; }

class Main {
    static void raise(Counter c) { ____; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Counter k = new Counter();
        k.n = 4;
        raise(k);
        System.out.print(k.n);
    }
}

A holder stores the cell object it is handed, and the caller compares the two names. What prints?
class Cell {
    int n;
    Cell(int n) { this.n = n; }
}

class Holder {
    Cell c;
    Holder(Cell c) { this.c = c; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cell a = new Cell(1);
        Holder h = new Holder(a);
        System.out.print(h.c == a);
    }
}

A getter hands back the cell object it holds, and the caller writes to what it receives. What prints?
class Cell {
    int n;
    Cell(int n) { this.n = n; }
}

class Box {
    Cell c = new Cell(2);
    Cell get() { return c; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Box x = new Box();
        Cell got = x.get();
        got.n = 8;
        System.out.print(x.c.n);
    }
}

A tally object hands back its number, and the caller saves that number before changing the object. What prints?
class Tally {
    int n = 4;
    int read() { return n; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Tally t = new Tally();
        int first = t.read();
        t.n = 8;
        System.out.print(first);
    }
}

A factory builds a box on every call, and the caller changes only the first result. What prints?
class Box {
    int n;
    Box(int n) { this.n = n; }
    static Box wrap() { return new Box(1); }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Box b = Box.wrap();
        b.n = 5;
        Box c = Box.wrap();
        System.out.print(c.n);
    }
}

A method builds a new cell from the current field of the cell it is called on, and the original is changed afterwards. What prints?
class Cell {
    int v;
    Cell(int v) { this.v = v; }
    Cell twice() { return new Cell(v * 2); }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cell a = new Cell(3);
        Cell b = a.twice();
        a.v = 10;
        System.out.print(b.v);
    }
}

A helper builds one cell from the values of two other cells. What integer prints? Respond with the integer only.
class Cell {
    int v;
    Cell(int v) { this.v = v; }
    static Cell span(Cell a, Cell b) { return new Cell(a.v + b.v); }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cell s = Cell.span(new Cell(2), new Cell(5));
        System.out.print(s.v);
    }
}

A method builds text from a field, and the caller saves that text before changing the field. What prints?
class Pt {
    int x;
    String show() { return "x=" + x; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Pt p = new Pt();
        p.x = 2;
        String s = p.show();
        p.x = 9;
        System.out.print(s);
    }
}
